(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-oxygen-delivery-vignettes"]={"title":"Oxygen Delivery Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a myocardial infarction. Her saturation is 93% on room air. A colleague wants to start oxygen by nasal cannula. Which is the right call?","choices":["Hold oxygen at a saturation of 93%","Start the nasal cannula at 2 L/min","Start a Venturi mask for her lung disease","Start a non-rebreather mask"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Oxygen is not given in a myocardial infarction unless the saturation is below 91%, because of vasoconstriction. Her saturation of 93% is above that, so oxygen is held. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 9, 15, 16"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man is placed on a non-rebreather mask at 10 L/min for severe hypoxia. The nurse notices the reservoir bag collapses completely each time he breathes in. Which dosing rule for this mask is not being met?","choices":["Prevent bag collapse on inspiration","Keep the flow between 1 and 6 L/min","Keep the fraction at or below 0.50","Add humidity at flows above 4 L/min"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The non-rebreather dosing rule is to prevent bag collapse on inspiration, at a flow of 10-15 L/min. His reservoir bag collapses completely each time he breathes in, so that rule is not being met, even though his 10 L/min is within the range. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 9, 11, 15, 17"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman on home oxygen has dry, cracked lips and uses a petroleum jelly lip balm several times a day. Which teaching applies?","choices":["Stop using petroleum-based products","Use more balm to protect the lips","Switch her oxygen to a non-rebreather","Stop her home oxygen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Petroleum-based products such as lip balms, lotions, oils and grease are to be avoided with home oxygen. Her petroleum jelly lip balm, used several times a day, is exactly that, so she should stop it. Her dry lips are not a reason to keep using it. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 7, 15"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with pneumonia is on a Venturi mask. She is breathing fast and deep, and her saturation dips each time she takes a big breath. Which dosing principle is being violated?","choices":["The fraction must stay under 0.50","Total flow must exceed inspiratory demand","The bag must not collapse on inspiration","Humidity is needed above 4 L/min"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The Venturi mask is an air-entrainment device that delivers a fixed fraction only if the total flow stays higher than her inspiratory demand. She is breathing fast and deep and her saturation dips with big breaths, which suggests her demand exceeds the flow. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with heart failure is on oxygen while his fluid overload is treated. Over two days his breathing eases, his saturation stays at target, and his vital signs settle. Which ongoing step is part of evaluating his oxygen therapy?","choices":["Keep monitoring for complications","Stop checking his saturation","Raise his flow to secure the gains","Start home oxygen qualification today"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Evaluating response ends with vital signs and further monitoring for complications. His breathing has eased and his saturation holds, but he remains on oxygen, so the hazards of oxygen therapy still need watching. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man admitted with a heart failure exacerbation still needs 2 L/min of oxygen on hospital day 1. His team plans to discharge him in a few days. When should his home oxygen qualification be performed?","choices":["Once he no longer needs oxygen","After a year on home oxygen","At his first outpatient visit","At the time of need, before discharge"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The qualification is documented at the time of need, and he still needs 2 L/min with discharge planned in a few days. So the qualification is performed while he is in the hospital, before he leaves, so that home oxygen can be arranged. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has attacks of severe one-sided headache around his eye, each lasting under an hour, several days in a row. His saturation is 99%. Which therapy is a listed special indication for him?","choices":["Nebulized saline","No oxygen, since he is not hypoxic","A bag-valve mask","Oxygen therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Severe one-sided headaches around the eye, brief and recurring daily for days, fit cluster headache, which is a listed special indication for oxygen. His saturation of 99% does not matter, because the indication here is the headache, not hypoxia. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with chronic lung disease drops from 94% to 86% while walking. Oxygen at 2 and then 4 L/min is added, but her saturation stays at 86% while walking. How does this result bear on ambulatory oxygen?","choices":["It qualifies her in Group I at rest","It qualifies her for sleep oxygen","It does not meet the exertion criterion","It qualifies her for ambulatory oxygen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Her saturation falls from 94% to 86% on walking, which is at or below 88%. But the exertion criterion also requires that the saturation improves when oxygen is given, and at 2 and 4 L/min it stays at 86%, so she does not meet the exertion criterion. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man with pneumonia is on a nasal cannula. His nurse turned the flow up to 8 L/min and his saturation is still 84%. He is alert and still ventilating well. Which is the next step?","choices":["Add a second nasal cannula","Lower the flow to 2 L/min","Change to a non-rebreather mask","Turn the cannula up to 12 L/min"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. His cannula is already above its limit: the cannula covers 1-6 L/min and its fraction tops out at 44%, so more turning gains nothing. He is still at 84% and still ventilating well, which is the listed use of the non-rebreather mask: severely hypoxic patients who are still ventilating well. At 10-15 L/min it delivers 60-100%, well above the cannula's ceiling. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 9, 11, 15"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man on home oxygen restores old cars in his garage and rubs grease off his hands with an oil-based cleanser while wearing his cannula. Which teaching applies?","choices":["Turn the flow up while working","Wear a simple mask in the garage","Use a Venturi mask when working","Avoid petroleum-based oils and grease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Home oxygen teaching is to avoid petroleum-based products such as lip balms, lotions, oils and grease. He is wearing his cannula while rubbing grease off with an oil-based cleanser, so he should avoid those petroleum oils and grease. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 7, 13, 16"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with a chronic lung disease flare needs both inhaled medication by nebulizer and supplemental oxygen. He has no tracheostomy. Which air-entrainment interface over his face suits him?","choices":["Nasal cannula","Aerosol mask","Trach collar","Non-rebreather mask"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. He needs a nebulizer for his inhaled medication along with oxygen, and he has no tracheostomy. The aerosol mask is an air-entrainment nebulizer interface that fits over the face, so it delivers oxygen and nebulized medication together. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man starting home oxygen says he smokes on his back porch and that his wife smokes in the kitchen. Which teaching applies?","choices":["Smoking is fine if the tank is turned off","He must stop; visitors are exempt","No one should smoke in the home","His wife may smoke if he is in another room"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Home oxygen teaching is that oxygen is highly flammable, and no one should smoke in the home. His wife smokes in the kitchen, inside the home, so the rule applies to her, and the fire danger is the same whoever lights the cigarette. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and cor pulmonale (right-sided heart failure from lung disease) has an arterial oxygen pressure of 58 mm Hg at rest. What is his qualification status?","choices":["Not qualified at 58 mm Hg","Qualified for sleep oxygen","Group II, recertified at 3 months","Group I, recertified at 12 months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. His arterial oxygen pressure of 58 mm Hg is above the Group I limit of 55 mm Hg but within the 56-59 range, which qualifies only with a comorbidity. He has cor pulmonale, so he is Group II, and Group II is recertified at 3 months. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man is found unresponsive and is not breathing. He has a pulse. A colleague reaches for a non-rebreather mask. Why does that device not fit his situation?","choices":["It is a high flow oxygen device","Its bag must collapse on each breath","It is for patients ventilating well","Its fraction of oxygen is too low"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. He is unresponsive and not breathing, though he has a pulse. A non-rebreather mask is used in severely hypoxic patients who are still ventilating well, and he is not breathing at all, so the mask's listed use does not fit him. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 15, 16, 17, 20"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with asthma is started on oxygen. She says she feels better, but her lips remain dusky, her accessory muscles are still working hard, and her saturation has not moved. How should her response be judged?","choices":["Adequate, because she feels better","Adequate, because oxygen is running","Cannot be judged without a chest film","Not yet adequate on clinical and objective measures"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. She feels better, but her lips are still dusky, her accessory muscles still work hard, and her saturation has not moved. Response is judged by symptoms, work of breathing, color and perfusion plus objective data. Most of those have not improved, so it is not yet adequate. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with chest pain has a myocardial infarction with ST elevation. He is alert, with an oxygen saturation of 96% on room air. Which is the right approach to oxygen?","choices":["Give a Venturi mask at 28%","Withhold oxygen at this saturation","Give a non-rebreather at 15 L/min","Give a nasal cannula at 2 L/min"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. He is alert with a saturation of 96% on room air, well above the 91% threshold, and oxygen is not given in a myocardial infarction unless the saturation is below 91%, because oxygen causes vasoconstriction. With no hypoxia, no oxygen is the right plan. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 11, 15, 16"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman going home on oxygen asks what signs to post. Which signage is taught?","choices":["A sign with her oxygen flow rate","A sign with her pharmacy's number","A sign with her recertification date","No Smoking and Oxygen in Use signs"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Home oxygen teaching is to post No Smoking signage, and potentially Oxygen in Use signs, within the home. Oxygen is highly flammable, and the signs warn everyone who enters, including visitors and firefighters. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man has a myocardial infarction and is placed on oxygen in the ambulance although his saturation was 98%. Which effect of oxygen makes this a problem?","choices":["Mucosal irritation","Fire risk","Vasoconstriction","Carbon dioxide retention"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. His saturation was 98%, so he was not hypoxic. Oxygen is not given in a myocardial infarction unless the saturation is below 91%, because oxygen causes vasoconstriction. Giving it to a man with a normal saturation exposes him to that effect without benefit. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 9"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with a severe exacerbation of his chronic lung disease is too short of breath to use his inhaler properly. Which delivery method is preferred for his inhaled medication?","choices":["Nebulizer","His usual inhaler, used faster","A trach collar","An oropharyngeal airway"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. He is in a severe exacerbation of chronic lung disease and too short of breath to use his inhaler properly. Inhalers are challenging in this situation, and a nebulizer delivers liquid medication directly to the airways without needing his coordination. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"An 84-year-old woman in hospice with metastatic cancer has air hunger (a distressing sense of not getting enough breath). Her saturation is 95%. Which therapy is a listed special indication?","choices":["Oxygen for symptom relief","No oxygen, since saturation is normal","A home oxygen qualification test","An endotracheal tube"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The hospice patient has air hunger, a distressing sense of not getting enough breath, and a saturation of 95%. Air hunger is a listed special indication, and symptom relief is a listed benefit of oxygen, so oxygen is given for comfort even without hypoxia. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 5, 21"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman has a daytime saturation of 95%. Overnight oximetry shows her falling to 91% at the lowest. Does she qualify for nocturnal oxygen?","choices":["No, her sleep value stays above 88%","Yes, since she drops during sleep","Yes, in Group II","Yes, if she also has diabetes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sleep qualification needs a saturation of 88% or less with a drop of 5% or more from baseline. Her lowest value of 91% is above 88%, and 95 minus 91 is only a 4% drop. She fails both parts, so she does not qualify. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is ready for discharge after his treatment is optimized. His resting saturation on room air is 87%, and he has no pulmonary hypertension, cor pulmonale (right-sided heart failure from lung disease) or high hematocrit. How often will his home oxygen be recertified?","choices":["Every 1 month","Every 3 months","Every 12 months","Every 24 months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. His resting room-air saturation is 87%, at or below 88%, which is the Group I qualification (conditional Group II needs a comorbidity such as pulmonary hypertension, cor pulmonale or a hematocrit above 56%). Group I patients are recertified every 12 months. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with heart failure is started on oxygen. An hour later her arterial oxygen pressure has risen, but her arterial carbon dioxide has also risen and her pH has fallen. How should this response be read?","choices":["A good response: pH is moving","Cannot be judged without a chest film","Not a good response: carbon dioxide is rising","A good response: oxygen pressure has risen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A good response needs the arterial oxygen pressure to rise without a rise in arterial carbon dioxide, along with correction of the pH. Hers rose, but so did her carbon dioxide while the pH fell, so the oxygen is not working as it should and she needs close reassessment. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is admitted with a flare. His oxygen saturation is 86% on room air, and the team wants to give him an exact, adjustable fraction of inspired oxygen rather than a fraction that changes with his breathing. Which delivery device is the best choice?","choices":["Simple mask","Venturi mask","Non-rebreather mask","Nasal cannula"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. He has chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and the team wants a precise, adjustable fraction of inspired oxygen that does not change with his breathing. The Venturi mask is an air-entrainment device: different ports set the fraction (24-50%), and it stays fixed whatever his demand. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with pneumonia is on a nasal cannula at 2 L/min. Her flow is increased to 5 L/min. About what fraction of inspired oxygen is she receiving now?","choices":["28%","40%","44%","60%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A nasal cannula at 2 L/min gives about 28%, and each added liter raises the fraction by about 4%. Going from 2 to 5 L/min adds 3 liters, which is 3 x 4% = 12%, so 28% + 12% = 40%. This matches the listed value of 40% at 5 L/min. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 9, 11, 14, 15"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with heart disease and type 2 diabetes has a resting room-air saturation of 89%. He has no pulmonary hypertension, no cor pulmonale (right-sided heart failure from lung disease), and his hematocrit is 44%. Does he qualify for home oxygen at rest?","choices":["No, 89% needs a listed comorbidity","Yes, in Group I","Yes, in Group II because of diabetes","Yes, because his hematocrit is high"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A resting saturation of 89% is above the 88% cutoff, so it needs a listed comorbidity: pulmonary hypertension, cor pulmonale or a hematocrit above 56%. He has none, and his hematocrit is only 44%. Heart disease and diabetes are not on the list, so he does not qualify at rest. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman on a nasal cannula at 6 L/min without humidification develops nosebleeds and a sore, crusted nose. Which hazard of oxygen therapy is this?","choices":["Hypercapnia","Vasoconstriction","Oxygen toxicity of the lungs","Mucosal irritation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. She has been on 6 L/min through a nasal cannula without humidification, and now has nosebleeds and a sore, crusted nose. Dry oxygen irritating the nasal lining is mucosal irritation, a listed hazard. Humidity is used when the flow is greater than 4 L/min. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 9"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman starting home oxygen loves candlelit dinners and keeps several candles on her dining table, where she sits with her concentrator tubing. Which teaching applies?","choices":["Candles are safe if the flow is low","Use scented candles instead","Keep the candles 2 feet away","Keep oxygen at least 10 feet from the candles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Home oxygen teaching is to keep oxygen at least 10 feet from heat sources, and candles are listed alongside pilot lights and electric appliances. She sits at the table with her concentrator tubing, so the candles must be at least 10 feet away. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and an echocardiogram showing pulmonary hypertension has a resting room-air saturation of 89%. Which statement about home oxygen applies to her?","choices":["She qualifies for sleep oxygen alone","She qualifies in Group II","She qualifies in Group I","She does not qualify at 89%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her resting saturation of 89% is above the Group I cutoff of 88% or less, so on its own it would not qualify. The echocardiogram showing pulmonary hypertension adds the comorbidity that makes a saturation of 89% qualify, which is Group II (recertified at 3 months). — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 4-year-old boy with wheezing cannot coordinate pressing a metered-dose inhaler with breathing in, even with coaching. Which delivery method is the rationale for his medication?","choices":["A Venturi mask","A nebulizer","A larger-dose inhaler","A non-rebreather mask"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A 4-year-old with wheezing cannot coordinate pressing a metered-dose inhaler with breathing in, even with coaching. Inhalers are challenging in certain situations, and a nebulizer delivers liquid medication directly to the airways without that coordination. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 24"}]};
